Title: "The Real Numbers"
Id: "d18bf879-7408-48c4-b09f-60d5fb20b183"


Text: "The real number system $\mathbb{R}$ extends the rational numbers to form
       a complete continuum. It is uniquely characterized up to isomorphism as a
       complete ordered field, ensuring that every bounded set of reals
       possesses a least upper bound (supremum)."
Id: "2f7605c2-086a-4429-a969-dd003a83531e"


SectionTitle: "The Real Field"
Id: "0d082d38-e717-4330-b29d-e67f0717341c"


[\reals]
Defines: Rb ::= (R, 0, 1, +, *, neg(x_), inv(x_), leq(a_, b_))
abstractly:
specifies:
. R is \set
. 0 "in" R
. 1 "in" R
. + is \binary.operation:on{R}
. * is \binary.operation:on{R}
. neg is \function:on{R}:to{R}
. inv is \function:on{R}:to{R}
. leq is \total.order:on{R}
expresses:
. 0 != 1
. inv(0) = 0
. forAll: a, b, c "in" R
  then:
  . (a + b) + c = a + (b + c)
  . a + b = b + a
  . a + 0 = a
  . a + neg(a) = 0
  . (a * b) * c = a * (b * c)
  . a * b = b * a
  . a * 1 = a
  . a * (b + c) = (a * b) + (a * c)
  . if: a != 0
    then: a * inv(a) = 1
  . if: leq(a, b)
    then: leq(a + c, b + c)
. forAll: a, b "in" R
  where:
  . leq(0, a)
  . leq(0, b)
  then: leq(0, a * b)
. forAll: A \:subset:/ R
  where:
  . exists: x "in" R
    suchThat: x "in" A
  . exists: M "in" R
    suchThat:
    . forAll: x "in" R
      where: x "in" A
      then: leq(x, M)
  then:
  . exists: s "in" R
    suchThat:
    . forAll: x "in" R
      where: x "in" A
      then: leq(x, s)
    . forAll: u "in" R
      where:
      . forAll: x "in" R
        where: x "in" A
        then: leq(x, u)
      then: leq(s, u)
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" Rb :-> x_ is \real
. view:
  as: S := R is \set
Documented:
. called: "the reals"
. written: "\mathbb{R}"
. description: "The real numbers $(\mathbb{R}, 0, 1, +, \cdot, \le)$ as a
                complete ordered field."
Id: "aeec4453-1166-47cd-a5f4-3eed295b3d15"


[\real]
Declares: x "in" \reals
Enables:
. capability: a_ + b_ :=> a_ \.real.+./ b_
. capability: a_ - b_ :=> a_ \.real.-./ b_
. capability: a_ * b_ :=> a_ \.real.*./ b_
. capability: a_ / b_ :=> a_ \.real.div./ b_
. capability: -a_ :=> \real.neg(a_)
Documented:
. called: "real"
. description: "A real number is a member of the underlying carrier set
                $\mathbb{R}$."
Id: "cc52bbbc-d925-469e-9252-2fad913ba14b"


[\real.zero]
Defines: z := \reals..0 is \real
Documented:
. called: "real zero"
. written: "0"
Id: "945650d9-8dab-4716-b4f7-2714e2219af9"


[\real.one]
Defines: o := \reals..1 is \real
Documented:
. called: "real one"
. written: "1"
Id: "1965d580-9b4d-4d19-93ee-82ebb75d1834"


[a_ \.real.+./ b_]
Defines: a_ + b_ := \reals..`+`(a_, b_) is \real
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ plus $b_?$"
. written: "a_? + b_?"
Id: "c6dc7fde-38f1-4e5b-a80a-2153567efe6f"


[a_ \.real.-./ b_]
Defines: a_ - b_ := a_ \.real.+./ \real.neg(b_) is \real
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ minus $b_?$"
. written: "a_? - b_?"
Id: "6daad43c-b638-4a51-b600-4c17a3d9a066"


[a_ \.real.*./ b_]
Defines: a_ * b_ := \reals..`*`(a_, b_) is \real
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ times $b_?$"
. written: "a_? \cdot b_?"
Id: "cde6b01e-d8c3-4996-b69a-1fdca9e69385"


[a_ \.real.div./ b_]
Defines: a_ / b_ := a_ \.real.*./ \real.inv(b_) is \real
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ divided by $b_?$"
. written: "\frac{a_?}{b_?}"
Id: "33d587f9-8ec0-4f27-8c65-42daae86748a"


[\real.neg(a_)]
Defines: n := \reals..neg(a_) is \real
Documented:
. called: "negative of $a_?$"
. written: "-a_?"
Id: "9b876af6-034f-4fb7-bc5f-af41345891fb"


[\real.inv(a_)]
Defines: i := \reals..inv(a_) is \real
Documented:
. called: "reciprocal of $a_?$"
. written: "a_?^{-1}"
Id: "87991f23-c868-4137-af54-b35e2e1d6067"


[a \:real.leq:/ b]
Declares: a is \real
when: b is \real
satisfies: \reals..leq(a, b)
Documented:
. called: "$a?$ is less than or equal to $b?$"
. written: "a? \leq b?"
Id: "d6f542fa-34a7-4359-9b32-78bb28af098a"


[a \:real.less:/ b]
Declares: a is \real
when: b is \real
satisfies:
. a \:real.leq?:/ b
. a != b
Documented:
. called: "$a?$ is less than $b?$"
. written: "a? < b?"
Id: "5b8f85c7-135a-4702-b5aa-1d61f526ae99"


[a \:real.geq:/ b]
Declares: a is \real
when: b is \real
satisfies: b \:real.leq?:/ a
Documented:
. called: "$a?$ is greater than or equal to $b?$"
. written: "a? \geq b?"
Id: "53068503-7473-4378-a2bd-d6aa98d1f9d5"


[a \:real.greater:/ b]
Declares: a is \real
when: b is \real
satisfies: b \:real.less?:/ a
Documented:
. called: "$a?$ is greater than $b?$"
. written: "a? > b?"
Id: "b0fef5ae-f1c8-46ab-b057-4000e9fda5d1"


SectionTitle: "Absolute Value and Bounds"
Id: "231d1127-646d-4845-88b1-f0ee9114a0a4"


[\real.abs(x_)]
Defines: a is \real
expresses:
. piecewise:
  if: x_ \:real.geq?:/ \real.zero
  then: a = x_
  else: a = \real.neg(x_)
Documented:
. called: "absolute value of $x_?$"
. written: "\left| x_? \right|"
. description: "The absolute value $|x| = x$ if $x \ge 0$ and $-x$ if $x < 0$."
Id: "f75ee806-0ba7-4585-8183-d7f6355ed4cc"


[\real.supremum:of{S}]
Defines: M is \real
when: S \:(nonempty)::subset:/ \reals
expresses:
. forAll: x is \real
  where: x "in" S
  then: x \:real.leq?:/ M
. forAll: M' is \real
  where:
  . forAll: x is \real
    where: x "in" S
    then: x \:real.leq?:/ M'
  then: M \:real.leq?:/ M'
Documented:
. called: "supremum of $S?$"
. written: "\sup(S?)"
. description: "The least upper bound (supremum) $\sup(S)$ of a set of real
                numbers."
Id: "d9762902-b78e-43a8-981d-47ec829c9552"


[\real.infimum:of{S}]
Defines: m is \real
when: S \:(nonempty)::subset:/ \reals
expresses:
. forAll: x is \real
  where: x "in" S
  then: m \:real.leq?:/ x
. forAll: m' is \real
  where:
  . forAll: x is \real
    where: x "in" S
    then: m' \:real.leq?:/ x
  then: m' \:real.leq?:/ m
Documented:
. called: "infimum of $S?$"
. written: "\inf(S?)"
. description: "The greatest lower bound (infimum) $\inf(S)$ of a set of real
                numbers."
Id: "181bf9bc-8b82-4962-9a70-1f64dd384ea7"


SectionTitle: "The Completeness Axiom"
Id: "04cc8ce6-8bdd-49d3-8e76-52dd47ef4e73"


Text: "The hallmark of the real numbers is Dedekind completeness: every nonempty
       subset that is bounded above has a supremum."
Id: "bc931815-1d01-4658-817f-efe57c5b2cf3"


[\completeness.of.reals]
Theorem:
given: S \:(nonempty)::subset:/ \reals
where:
. exists: M is \real
  suchThat:
  . forAll: x is \real
    where: x "in" S
    then: x \:real.leq?:/ M
then:
. exists: sup is \real
  suchThat: sup = \real.supremum:of{S}
Documented:
. called: "Completeness of the Real Numbers"
. description: "Every nonempty set of real numbers bounded above has a least
                upper bound in $\mathbb{R}$."
Id: "b4fc64c6-378b-4e44-87c5-610045a90fe3"


SectionTitle: "Realization by Cauchy Sequences"
Id: "92086a5a-de67-4ccf-bbd7-dfb720e13253"


Text: "A concrete real is an equivalence class of Cauchy sequences of rational
       numbers. Two sequences are equivalent when their difference converges to
       zero. Constants and arithmetic are induced term by term; reciprocal is
       applied to a representative of a nonzero class, with the zero class
       handled separately."
Id: "4b60c150-1dcd-4f73-ab04-d1de404930a6"


[\rational.sequence]
Declares: A ::= a(n_) ::= q_
specifies:
. n_ is \natural
. q_ is \rational
Documented:
. called: "rational sequence"
. writing: a(n)
  as: "a?_{n?}"
. description: "A sequence of rational numbers indexed by the naturals."
Id: "94c86080-9c65-4b25-bbba-ee56fdfee3a4"


[\cauchy.rational.sequence]
Declares: A ::= a(n_) ::= q_
specifies:
. n_ is \natural
. q_ is \rational
satisfies:
. forAll: epsilon is \rational
  where: \rational.zero \:rational.less?:/ epsilon
  then:
  . exists: N is \natural
    suchThat:
    . forAll: m, n is \natural
      where:
      . N \:natural.leq?:/ m
      . N \:natural.leq?:/ n
      then: \rational.abs(a(m) \.rational.-./ a(n)) \:rational.less?:/ epsilon
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy rational sequence"
. description: "A rational sequence is Cauchy when its terms eventually lie
                within every positive rational distance of one another."
Id: "db1721ae-e0d4-4421-abc3-a586c0865ea5"


[\rational.cauchy.sequences]
Defines: C is \set
expresses:
. let: A is \\anything
  then:
  . have: A "in"? C
    iff: A is? \cauchy.rational.sequence
Enables:
. capability: A_ "in" C :<->: A_ is \cauchy.rational.sequence
Documented:
. called: "rational Cauchy sequences"
. written: "\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{Q})"
Id: "b77feec6-c586-452f-804e-6ec2b591ac1f"


[\real.cauchy.relation]
Defines: E is \equivalence.relation:on{\rational.cauchy.sequences}
expresses:
. forAll: A ::= a(n_) ::= qa_ is \cauchy.rational.sequence
  then:
  . forAll: B ::= b(n_) ::= qb_ is \cauchy.rational.sequence
    then:
    . have: E(A, B)
      iff:
      . forAll: epsilon is \rational
        where: \rational.zero \:rational.less?:/ epsilon
        then:
        . exists: N is \natural
          suchThat:
          . forAll: n is \natural
            where: N \:natural.leq?:/ n
            then: \rational.abs(a(n) \.rational.-./ b(n)) \:rational.less?:/ epsilon
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy-sequence equivalence"
. description: "Two rational Cauchy sequences are equivalent when their termwise
                difference converges to zero."
Id: "24cd9c1f-5185-495b-8107-e9cce4d5dc76"


[\real.cauchy.carrier]
Defines: R := \rational.cauchy.sequences \.mod./ \real.cauchy.relation is \set
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy real carrier"
. written: "\mathcal{C}(\mathbb{Q})/\sim"
Id: "61d7d021-3531-419a-b33e-ec2d2286106f"


[\real.cauchy.class:of{A ::= a(n_)}]
Defines: X := \equivalence.class:of{A}:over{\real.cauchy.relation}#using{X := \rational.cauchy.sequences} "in" \real.cauchy.carrier
when: A is \cauchy.rational.sequence
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy-real class of $A?$"
. written: "[a?]_{\mathbb{R}}"
Id: "ec3f6cc5-a1e6-4a36-8001-9e06b1270512"


[\rational.constant.cauchy.sequence:of{q}]
Defines: C ::= c(n_) is \cauchy.rational.sequence
when: q is \rational
expresses: c(n_) := q
Documented:
. called: "constant rational Cauchy sequence at $q?$"
. written: "(q?)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}"
Id: "c195139a-50b8-480b-944a-bd5db899eee6"


[\rational.cauchy.sequence.add{A ::= a(n_), B ::= b(n_)}]
Defines: C ::= c(n_) is \cauchy.rational.sequence
when:
. A is \cauchy.rational.sequence
. B is \cauchy.rational.sequence
expresses: c(n_) := a(n_) \.rational.+./ b(n_)
Documented:
. called: "termwise sum of $A?$ and $B?$"
. description: "The Cauchy sequence whose $n$th term is $a_n+b_n$."
Id: "49fd2bc3-3793-4f70-a9ca-6e916aeda3b7"


[\rational.cauchy.sequence.mul{A ::= a(n_), B ::= b(n_)}]
Defines: C ::= c(n_) is \cauchy.rational.sequence
when:
. A is \cauchy.rational.sequence
. B is \cauchy.rational.sequence
expresses: c(n_) := a(n_) \.rational.*./ b(n_)
Documented:
. called: "termwise product of $A?$ and $B?$"
. description: "The Cauchy sequence whose $n$th term is $a_nb_n$."
Id: "bf36574e-aa8c-48b2-b63c-2e563d34630b"


[\rational.cauchy.sequence.neg{A ::= a(n_)}]
Defines: C ::= c(n_) is \cauchy.rational.sequence
when: A is \cauchy.rational.sequence
expresses: c(n_) := \rational.neg(a(n_))
Documented:
. called: "termwise negative of $A?$"
. description: "The Cauchy sequence whose $n$th term is $-a_n$."
Id: "05a28bdc-80ba-4598-8a32-08ad2c53cf13"


[\rational.cauchy.sequence.inv{A ::= a(n_)}]
Defines: C ::= c(n_) is \cauchy.rational.sequence
when: A is \(nonzero)::cauchy.rational.sequence
expresses: c(n_) := \rational.inv(a(n_))
Documented:
. called: "termwise reciprocal of $A?$"
. description: "For a nonzero Cauchy-real class, its representative is
                eventually bounded away from zero, so termwise reciprocal gives
                another Cauchy sequence. Finitely many zero terms are sent to
                zero by the totalized rational reciprocal."
Id: "9b4a6a46-43f6-41b2-9bac-bffb8fc6dcce"


[\(nonzero)::cauchy.rational.sequence]
Refines: A
satisfies: \real.cauchy.class:of{A} != \real.cauchy.zero
Documented:
. adjective: "nonzero"
. description: "A rational Cauchy sequence whose real equivalence class is not
                the zero class."
Id: "6d5bf51a-cbb7-4b8b-8fc3-895af897a8a7"


[\real.cauchy.zero]
Defines: z := \real.cauchy.class:of{\rational.constant.cauchy.sequence:of{\rational.zero}} "in" \real.cauchy.carrier
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy-real zero"
. written: "[(0,0,\ldots)]"
Id: "ae2dfbd0-f125-4337-8a38-beb0f6f32385"


[\real.cauchy.one]
Defines: o := \real.cauchy.class:of{\rational.constant.cauchy.sequence:of{\rational.one}} "in" \real.cauchy.carrier
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy-real one"
. written: "[(1,1,\ldots)]"
Id: "a631942f-fad8-4589-8b48-2564aca96f97"


[\real.cauchy.add]
Defines: add(x_, y_) is \binary.operation:on{\real.cauchy.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll: A ::= a(n_) ::= qa_ is \cauchy.rational.sequence
  then:
  . forAll: B ::= b(n_) ::= qb_ is \cauchy.rational.sequence
    then: add(\real.cauchy.class:of{A}, \real.cauchy.class:of{B}) = \real.cauchy.class:of{\rational.cauchy.sequence.add{A, B}}
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy-real addition"
. description: "Addition induced by termwise addition of representative rational
                Cauchy sequences."
Id: "258c8289-202b-4fd4-a228-2a63bd2e47f0"


[\real.cauchy.mul]
Defines: mul(x_, y_) is \binary.operation:on{\real.cauchy.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll: A ::= a(n_) ::= qa_ is \cauchy.rational.sequence
  then:
  . forAll: B ::= b(n_) ::= qb_ is \cauchy.rational.sequence
    then: mul(\real.cauchy.class:of{A}, \real.cauchy.class:of{B}) = \real.cauchy.class:of{\rational.cauchy.sequence.mul{A, B}}
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy-real multiplication"
. description: "Multiplication induced by termwise multiplication of
                representative rational Cauchy sequences."
Id: "d561067f-65ed-4123-9601-3fdb817dda0a"


[\real.cauchy.neg(x_)]
Defines: neg(x_) is \function:on{\real.cauchy.carrier}:to{\real.cauchy.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll: A ::= a(n_) ::= qa_ is \cauchy.rational.sequence
  then: neg(\real.cauchy.class:of{A}) = \real.cauchy.class:of{\rational.cauchy.sequence.neg{A}}
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy-real negation"
. description: "Negation induced by termwise rational negation."
Id: "4d5c310d-b088-46c8-836b-69aa22c5072e"


[\real.cauchy.inv(x_)]
Defines: inv(x_) is \function:on{\real.cauchy.carrier}:to{\real.cauchy.carrier}
expresses:
. inv(\real.cauchy.zero) = \real.cauchy.zero
. forAll: A ::= a(n_) ::= qa_ is \(nonzero)::cauchy.rational.sequence
  then: inv(\real.cauchy.class:of{A}) = \real.cauchy.class:of{\rational.cauchy.sequence.inv{A}}
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy-real reciprocal"
. description: "The reciprocal induced by termwise reciprocal on nonzero
                classes, with zero sent to zero."
Id: "b7a4d100-4eeb-4e9c-a332-6b3ec447991a"


[\real.cauchy.leq]
Defines: leq(x_, y_) is \total.order:on{\real.cauchy.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll: A ::= a(n_) ::= qa_ is \cauchy.rational.sequence
  then:
  . forAll: B ::= b(n_) ::= qb_ is \cauchy.rational.sequence
    then:
    . have: leq(\real.cauchy.class:of{A}, \real.cauchy.class:of{B})
      iff:
      . forAll: epsilon is \rational
        where: \rational.zero \:rational.less?:/ epsilon
        then:
        . exists: N is \natural
          suchThat:
          . forAll: n is \natural
            where: N \:natural.leq?:/ n
            then:
            . let: r is \rational
              where: r = b(n) \.rational.+./ epsilon
              then: a(n) \:rational.leq?:/ r
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy-real order"
. description: "The quotient order: $[a]\le[b]$ when $a_n$ is eventually no more
                than an arbitrary positive rational above $b_n$."
Id: "dc1bc46f-e4ed-4968-b4f9-42abbe838ec3"


[\real.cauchy.realization]
Realizes: Rb ::= (R, 0, 1, +, *, neg(x_), inv(x_), leq(a_, b_)) := \reals
specifies:
. R := \real.cauchy.carrier
. 0 := \real.cauchy.zero
. 1 := \real.cauchy.one
. + := \real.cauchy.add
. * := \real.cauchy.mul
. neg := \real.cauchy.neg
. inv := \real.cauchy.inv
. leq := \real.cauchy.leq
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" Rb :<->: x_ "in" R
Documented:
. called: "reals as Cauchy sequences"
. description: "The concrete realization of the abstract real tuple as
                equivalence classes of rational Cauchy sequences, with every
                field operation induced from sequence operations."
Id: "b03848a9-e6b1-4bca-8436-8dd755e5a026"


SectionTitle: "Realization by Dedekind Cuts"
Id: "783f1fa2-7254-441a-8e24-41c10acee7d8"


Text: "A Dedekind cut is a nonempty proper lower subset of the rationals with no
       greatest element. Every Cauchy-real class determines the cut of rationals
       lying strictly below its limit. The canonical correspondence is
       bijective, so the Cauchy operations above can be transported across it to
       give the cut carrier the same complete ordered-field structure."
Id: "b6ed3b04-895c-4c2f-af12-9a5d670e2a66"


[\dedekind.cut]
Declares: D \:subset:/ \rationals
satisfies:
. exists: q is \rational
  suchThat: q "in" D
. exists: r is \rational
  suchThat:
  . not: r "in"? D
. forAll: x is \rational
  where: x "in" D
  then:
  . forAll: y is \rational
    where: y \:rational.less?:/ x
    then: y "in" D
. forAll: x is \rational
  where: x "in" D
  then:
  . exists: y is \rational
    suchThat:
    . y "in" D
    . x \:rational.less?:/ y
Documented:
. called: "Dedekind cut"
. description: "A nonempty proper downward-closed subset of $\mathbb{Q}$ with no
                greatest member."
Id: "7b25c7dc-7d0f-4737-a7f0-fb130244ea57"


[\dedekind.cut.carrier]
Defines: D := \set@{C_ : C_ is \dedekind.cut} is \set
Documented:
. called: "Dedekind-cut carrier"
. written: "\mathcal{D}(\mathbb{Q})"
Id: "760a04d0-6a51-432c-8e32-830b8614c5cc"


[\dedekind.from.cauchy(x_)]
Defines: cut(x_) is \(bijective)::function:on{\real.cauchy.carrier}:to{\dedekind.cut.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll: A ::= a(n_) ::= qa_ is \cauchy.rational.sequence
  then:
  . forAll: q is \rational
    then:
    . let: C is \dedekind.cut
      where: C = cut(\real.cauchy.class:of{A})
      then:
      . have: q "in"? C
        iff:
        . exists: epsilon is \rational
          suchThat:
          . \rational.zero \:rational.less?:/ epsilon
          . exists: N is \natural
            suchThat:
            . forAll: n is \natural
              where: N \:natural.leq?:/ n
              then:
              . let: r is \rational
                where: r = q \.rational.+./ epsilon
                then:
                . let: s is \rational
                  where: s = a(n)
                  then: r \:rational.less?:/ s
Documented:
. called: "Dedekind cut of a Cauchy real"
. description: "The canonical bijection sending a Cauchy-real class to the lower
                set of rationals that remain a positive rational distance below
                a representative sequence."
Id: "28fd2c5f-4aec-46ad-bdfe-557bd5e0a157"


[\cauchy.from.dedekind(D_)]
Defines: c(D_) is \function:on{\dedekind.cut.carrier}:to{\real.cauchy.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll: x "in" \real.cauchy.carrier
  then: c(\dedekind.from.cauchy(x)) = x
. forAll: E "in" \dedekind.cut.carrier
  then: \dedekind.from.cauchy(c(E)) = E
Documented:
. called: "Cauchy real of a Dedekind cut"
. description: "The inverse of the canonical Cauchy-real to Dedekind-cut
                bijection."
Id: "c4155cd8-b3e0-4b9b-839a-62713fb57eef"


[\dedekind.zero]
Defines: z := \dedekind.from.cauchy(\real.cauchy.zero) "in" \dedekind.cut.carrier
Documented:
. called: "Dedekind-cut zero"
Id: "144c236e-d857-4dd6-b24f-d75c9fa3abfa"


[\dedekind.one]
Defines: o := \dedekind.from.cauchy(\real.cauchy.one) "in" \dedekind.cut.carrier
Documented:
. called: "Dedekind-cut one"
Id: "5f0b2c73-9459-4fa0-9a85-fa405eccfbda"


[\dedekind.add]
Defines: add(D_, E_) is \binary.operation:on{\dedekind.cut.carrier}
expresses: add(D_, E_) := \dedekind.from.cauchy(\real.cauchy.realization..`+`(\cauchy.from.dedekind(D_), \cauchy.from.dedekind(E_)))
Documented:
. called: "Dedekind-cut addition"
. description: "Addition transported from the Cauchy-sequence realization."
Id: "92bdddcd-71a4-40fc-84aa-669a4f3d9671"


[\dedekind.mul]
Defines: mul(D_, E_) is \binary.operation:on{\dedekind.cut.carrier}
expresses: mul(D_, E_) := \dedekind.from.cauchy(\real.cauchy.realization..`*`(\cauchy.from.dedekind(D_), \cauchy.from.dedekind(E_)))
Documented:
. called: "Dedekind-cut multiplication"
. description: "Multiplication transported from the Cauchy-sequence realization."
Id: "c4d0e237-a2a2-4b17-bb5e-a50b43798af3"


[\dedekind.neg(D_)]
Defines: neg(D_) is \function:on{\dedekind.cut.carrier}:to{\dedekind.cut.carrier}
expresses: neg(D_) := \dedekind.from.cauchy(\real.cauchy.neg(\cauchy.from.dedekind(D_)))
Documented:
. called: "Dedekind-cut negation"
. description: "Negation transported from the Cauchy-sequence realization."
Id: "72cd1d99-ebf2-4d27-91a6-377b5be4e7b5"


[\dedekind.inv(D_)]
Defines: inv(D_) is \function:on{\dedekind.cut.carrier}:to{\dedekind.cut.carrier}
expresses: inv(D_) := \dedekind.from.cauchy(\real.cauchy.inv(\cauchy.from.dedekind(D_)))
Documented:
. called: "Dedekind-cut reciprocal"
. description: "The totalized reciprocal transported from the Cauchy-sequence
                realization."
Id: "cda76fde-d83d-4dc7-a3e5-59a11d4a4111"


[\dedekind.leq]
Defines: leq(D_, E_) is \total.order:on{\dedekind.cut.carrier}
expresses: leq(D_, E_) := \real.cauchy.realization..leq(\cauchy.from.dedekind(D_), \cauchy.from.dedekind(E_))
Documented:
. called: "Dedekind-cut order"
. description: "The total order transported from the Cauchy-sequence
                realization; equivalently, it is inclusion of lower cuts."
Id: "a1a11645-dd09-42f8-aaa4-b44588aed399"


[\real.dedekind.realization]
Realizes: Rb ::= (R, 0, 1, +, *, neg(x_), inv(x_), leq(a_, b_)) := \reals
specifies:
. R := \dedekind.cut.carrier
. 0 := \dedekind.zero
. 1 := \dedekind.one
. + := \dedekind.add
. * := \dedekind.mul
. neg := \dedekind.neg
. inv := \dedekind.inv
. leq := \dedekind.leq
Enables:
. capability: D_ "in" Rb :<->: D_ "in" R
Documented:
. called: "reals as Dedekind cuts"
. description: "The concrete realization of the abstract real tuple by Dedekind
                cuts, with its constants, operations, and order transported from
                the Cauchy-sequence realization."
Id: "f1d4ea17-820a-436f-99e4-a41ee4b7ae9b"


SectionTitle: "Consequences of Completeness"
Id: "c7e3ce84-b112-44c7-aecd-7903f25f6f48"


TextTheorem: "Archimedean property. For every real number $x$ there exists a
              natural number $n$ with $n>x$. Equivalently, for every
              $\epsilon>0$ there exists $n\in\mathbb{N}$ with $0<1/n<\epsilon$.
              Although this property is often treated as obvious, it follows
              from completeness and excludes infinitely large or positive
              infinitesimal real numbers."
Id: "b1263e32-5964-4429-bd0c-7ed965ffa9a1"


TextTheorem: "The rational numbers are dense in the real numbers: if $x<y$,
              there exists $q\in\mathbb{Q}$ with $x<q<y$. The irrational numbers
              are also dense. Density says that every nonempty open interval
              meets each subset; it does not say that the subsets have equal
              cardinality or measure."
Id: "fad49d51-ff50-4127-806b-8f246765c4df"


TextTheorem: "Nested interval theorem. If
              $[a_1,b_1]\supseteq[a_2,b_2]\supseteq\cdots$ is a decreasing
              sequence of nonempty closed bounded intervals, then their
              intersection is nonempty. If $b_n-a_n\to0$, the intersection
              contains exactly one point. This is another operational form of
              completeness."
Id: "b160143e-4ba6-481c-9a52-3e03fdee7bc5"


TextTheorem: "There is, up to a unique order-preserving field isomorphism, one
              complete ordered field. Thus constructions by Dedekind cuts and by
              Cauchy sequences yield isomorphic ordered fields. The realization
              changes; the real-number structure does not."
Id: "59879fc7-468d-4926-8c40-43fe85a7713b"
